      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make unusual-looking powers feel ordinary.\n\nAn exponent can be a fraction, a negative number, or a decimal.\n\nThe book handles all three by rewriting them into familiar roots and reciprocals. We will keep the numbers chosen so every answer can be found exactly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "concept_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "concept_1": {
      "id": "concept_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The index 1/n means an nth root.\n\n64^(1/3) = cube root of 64 = 4, because 4^3 = 64.\n\nLikewise, 81^(1/4) is the fourth root of 81.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
