    "recap": {
      "id": "recap",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Fractional index → root.\n\na^(1/n) = nth root of a.\na^(m/n) means take the nth root and use the mth power.\nA negative exponent means reciprocal.\nA terminating decimal exponent can often be changed to a simple fraction first.\n\nExplain why 16^(-1/2) is 1/4, not -4.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "finish"
        }
      ]
    },
    "finish": {
      "id": "finish",
      "type": "COMPLETION",
      "content": {
        "text": "Good. When an exponent looks unfamiliar, rewrite the exponent before touching the base. Ask: is it a root, a reciprocal, or both? That one habit prevents most sign and root mistakes.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
